THIRD PROVENANCE — public standards, read directly
Covers:    the substance of accessibility.md, spacing.md, layout.md,
           microcopy.md and icons.md, and the citations in typography.md.
           Neither upstream held usable material for spacing, layout, microcopy
           or icons.
Sources:   WCAG 2.2 success criteria with their conformance levels (w3.org);
           Apple Human Interface Guidelines; Material Design; IBM Carbon;
           GitHub Primer; GOV.UK Design System; Bringhurst, The Elements of
           Typographic Style.
Rule:      where a figure comes from one design system rather than from a
           standard, the text says which. A model reading these files would
           otherwise repeat a convention as a requirement.

VERIFICATION
Every claim carrying a clause number or an attribution was checked against its
primary source before shipping. Several inherited claims did not survive: the
uppercase-tracking floor upstream states as required rests on a Bringhurst
passage about strings of small and full capitals, in which he writes that
display capitals have no generalised optimum; an easing curve attributed to
Material was wrong; and Arabic size and line-height ranges had no traceable
source and were removed.

The rules name no palette, because the output lands in the user's project
rather than in ours. Concrete hex values appear only as values to avoid.
